<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Android Auto Backup, for Android 11 and below (API ≤ 30). The API 31+ rules
  live next door in data_extraction_rules.xml and have to say the same thing;
  both are declared on <application> because the platform picks by version.

  What is excluded is the async preference store, which on Android is a
  DataStore file under files/datastore/ (SharedPreferencesAsync, not the legacy
  SharedPreferences file). It holds the notification-invitation record, and
  that record must not travel: permission is granted per install, so a restored
  phone would arrive claiming the question had been answered there when it had
  not — and the platform, which does not restore permission, would never be
  asked again. See NotificationInvitationStore and resolveAppGate.

  The exclusion is the whole directory rather than the one file, because
  DataStore writes through a sibling .tmp and the file name is the plugin's to
  choose. The consequence is worth writing down: *every* preference this app
  keeps in SharedPreferencesAsync is device-local across a restore. If one is
  ever added that should survive one, it needs its own store rather than a
  narrowing of this rule.

  The local SQLite database is deliberately NOT excluded — the garden should
  come back on a new phone. That is the whole reason this is a rule rather
  than android:allowBackup="false".
-->
<full-backup-content>
  <exclude domain="file" path="datastore/" />
</full-backup-content>
